              },
              "summary": "Whether generated power firmware talks to an on-target monitor."
            },
            {
              "description": "Whether this run asks for host-gated *external* power capture.\n\nThe single source of the predicate that gates every piece of GPIO sync\nmachinery: the firmware's ``kPowerSyncEnabled`` (via\n``SyncContext.power_sync_enabled`` and the NSX GPIO module selection in\n``firmware/__init__.py``) and the host-side lock-step default below.\nInternal (on-device monitor) mode measures inside the firmware and has\nno host poller to race, so it is excluded.",
              "examples": [],
              "id": "helia_profiler.PowerConfig.gated_external_capture",
              "kind": "attribute",
              "language": "python",
              "members": [],
              "name": "gated_external_capture",
              "params": [],
              "raises": [],
              "returns": [],
              "signature": "gated_external_capture: bool",
              "source": {
                "line": 288,
                "path": "src/helia_profiler/config/power.py",
                "url": "https://github.com/AmbiqAI/helia-profiler/blob/__DOCS_SOURCE_REF__/src/helia_profiler/config/power.py#L288"
              },
              "summary": "Whether this run asks for host-gated external power capture."
            },
            {
              "description": "Whether the board carries the two extra lock-step wires.\n\n``state`` (device -> host) and ``go`` (host -> device); ``0`` means the\nwire is not assigned. Single-sourced because three consumers ask the\nsame question and must agree: the lock-step default\n(:attr:`lockstep_resolved`), the ``power.lockstep: true`` config\nvalidator, and the ``no_gate_rise`` diagnostic, which only names\nlock-step as the likely fix when the wiring can actually support it.",
              "examples": [],
              "id": "helia_profiler.PowerConfig.lockstep_wiring_available",
              "kind": "attribute",
              "language": "python",
              "members": [],
              "name": "lockstep_wiring_available",
              "params": [],
              "raises": [],
              "returns": [],
              "signature": "lockstep_wiring_available: bool",
              "source": {
                "line": 301,
                "path": "src/helia_profiler/config/power.py",
                "url": "https://github.com/AmbiqAI/helia-profiler/blob/__DOCS_SOURCE_REF__/src/helia_profiler/config/power.py#L301"
              },
              "summary": "Whether the board carries the two extra lock-step wires."
            },
            {
              "description": "Effective 3-wire GPIO lock-step decision for this run.\n\nAn explicit ``power.lockstep`` always wins -- auto-enable is a\n*default*, never an override, so ``lockstep: false`` still forces the\nfree-running path for bring-up on incomplete wiring.\n\nLeft unset, lock-step is enabled whenever the board is wired for it and\ngated external capture is requested. The hazard it closes is not\nfamily-specific: without lock-step ``kSyncLockstep`` bakes false,\n``hpx_sync_wait_go()`` compiles to a no-op, and the target free-runs its\nmeasured window straight out of reset. Any reset latency the host\nspends after that -- flash-tool exit, JLinkExe teardown, poller\nstart-up -- races the gate. Apollo5's default\n``debug_reset+swpoi_reset`` widens the gap most (two sequential\nJLinkExe invocations), but every family can reproduce the same\n``no_gate_rise`` degradation (issue #114). The condition is the\nwiring and the mode, not the SoC family.\n\nThis is the one place both the firmware generator (which bakes\n``kSyncLockstep`` in at build time, via ``FirmwareRenderContext``) and\nthe host-side capture path (which must arm/wait/signal accordingly)\nresolve the *same* answer -- callers must not read\n:attr:`lockstep` directly.",
              "examples": [],
